'''Yalarnnga''' (also ''Jalarnnga'', ''Jalanga'', ''Yelina'', ''Yellunga'', ''Yellanga'', ''Yalarrnnga'', ''Yalanga'' or ''Yalluna''<ref name="Breen"/>) is an [[language death|extinct]] [[Australian Aboriginal language]] of the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan]] [[language family]], that may be related to the [[Kalkatungu language]].<ref name="AIATSIS" /><ref name=":SLQa">{{SLQ-CC-BY|url=https://maps.slq.qld.gov.au/iyil/view/171?embed=true|title=Yulluna|author(s)=[[State Library of Queensland]]|accessdate=20 May 2022}}</ref> It was formerly spoken by the [[Yalarnnga|Yalarnnga people]] in areas near the [[Gulf of Carpentaria]] the towns of [[Dajarra]] and [[Cloncurry, Queensland|Cloncurry]] in far northwestern [[Queensland]].<ref name=":SLQa" /><ref name=":SLQ">{{SLQ-CC-BY|url=https://maps.slq.qld.gov.au/iyil/view/159?embed=true|title=Yalarnnga|author(s)=[[State Library of Queensland]]|accessdate=16 May 2022}}</ref> The last native speaker died in 1980.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last1=Breen |first1=Gavan |url=https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/handle/1885/146740 |title=The grammar of Yalarnnga : a language of western Queensland |last2=Blake |first2=Barry J. |publisher=Pacific Linguistics, Research School of Pacific and Asian Studies, The Australian National University |year=2007 |isbn=978-0-85883-567-2}}</ref> It is a suffixing [[agglutinative language]] with no attested prefixes.<ref name="Breen">{{cite book |last1=Breen |first1=Gavan |title=The grammar of Yalarnnga : a language of western Queensland |date=2007 |publisher=Pacific Linguistics, Research School of Pacific and Asian Studies, the Australian National University |location=Canberra, ACT |isbn=978-0858835672}}</ref>

== Classification ==
Yalarnnga is sometimes grouped with [[Kalkatungu language|Kalkatungu]] as the [[Kalkatungic languages|Kalkatungic]] (Galgadungic) branch of the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan]] family. O'Grady et al.,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=O'Grady |first1=Geoffrey N. |last2=Voegelin |first2=Charles F. |last3=Voegelin |first3=Florence M. |date=1966 |title=Languages of the World: Indo-Pacific Fascicle Six |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/30029431.pdf |journal=[[Anthropological Linguistics (journal)|Anthropological Linguistics]] |volume=8 |issue=2 |pages=1–197 |jstor=30029431 }}</ref> however, classify Kalkatungu as the sole member of the "Kalkatungic group" of the Pama-Nyungan family, and Dixon (2002)<ref>{{Cite book |last=Dixon |first=R. M. W. |url=https://www.cambridge.org/au/academic/subjects/languages-linguistics/other-languages-and-linguistics/australian-languages-their-nature-and-development?format=HB&isbn=9780521473781 |title=Australian Languages: Their Nature and Development |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |year=2003 |isbn=9780521473781 |location=Cambridge |author-link=Robert M. W. Dixon}}</ref> regards Kalkatungic as an [[Sprachbund|areal group]].

